<<<
via, via. In via media, via is the Latin nominative, & must not have -d. In the use as a preposition
===
**via, viâ.** In *via media*, *via* is the Latin nominative, & must not have *-â*. In the use as a preposition
>>>

<<<
through ’ it is the Latin ablative, the distinguishing of which by a circum flex accent is optional. As both forms are pronounced vi’a, & there is never any risk of confusion, it seems idle to retain the accent, but
===
through ' it is the Latin ablative, the distinguishing of which by a circumflex accent is optional. As both forms are pronounced vī'*a*, & there is never any risk of confusion, it seems idle to retain the accent, but
>>>

